# Working on `{{APP}}`

This file is written by AppLab every time source is uploaded to the app
`{{APP}}`. Editing it here changes nothing: the next push overwrites it. It
describes how to drive this app's whole lifecycle; what differs between apps is
the app's id, and the address and key written into `applab.sh`.

## What this app is

An AppLab app is one source repository, one image, one Deployment and one
address. This repository **is** the app's source: uploading to it is how code
gets in, and what is committed is what can be deployed.

The app is served at a URL the deployment knows. Ask for it:

    curl -sS "$APPLAB_URL/api/v1/apps/{{APP}}/status"

## The two things you need

Both are filled in at the top of `applab.sh`, so a fresh clone runs with nothing
exported:

    APPLAB_URL   the deployment, e.g. https://applab.example.com
    APPLAB_KEY   this app's key

`APPLAB_URL` is the base address of the AppLab that manages this app — the same
address its console is served at. `GET $APPLAB_URL/api/v1/describe` needs no key
and reports it, along with every endpoint that exists.

`APPLAB_KEY` is the credential for **this app**. It reaches this app and nothing
else: upload, build, deploy, roll back, read logs, change configuration. It
cannot delete the app and cannot see any other app on the platform.

Both are written in as *defaults*, not as values: each is read as
`${APPLAB_URL:-...}`, so exporting either one wins. That is what working against
a second deployment, or with a key you rotated, takes.

**The repository carries the key, and the repository is not private.** It is
cloned, uploaded, built into an image and mirrored, so the key goes with every
copy — into an image layer too, if this directory is what gets built. That is a
deliberate trade: it is what makes a fresh clone work with no setup. It is
recoverable, because an admin can replace the key with `applab keys rotate
{{APP}}` and the next upload writes the new one in. Treat a copy of this
repository as a copy of the credential. A value the app needs *at runtime*
belongs in its environment or secret configuration instead — `./applab.sh env
set` and `./applab.sh secret set` — which is applied on the next deploy and never
enters the source.

## `./applab.sh`

The repository carries a script that does all of this with nothing but curl.
It reads the two variables above, so it needs no arguments beyond the command.
Beyond curl it uses only sed, tar and what a POSIX shell and a base system ship —
nothing to install. The header of `applab.sh` lists every command it calls.

    ./applab.sh push              # upload, build, deploy — the whole path
    ./applab.sh status            # what is running, and where
    ./applab.sh logs -f           # watch it
    ./applab.sh diagnose          # why it is not working, if it is not
    ./applab.sh history           # commits you can deploy or roll back to
    ./applab.sh rollback <commit> # go back to an earlier one
    ./applab.sh help              # everything else

`push` is the normal path. The rest are for when something needs looking at.

It does everything the app's page in the console does — the two are the same
operations against the same API:

    ./applab.sh show              # port, replicas, domain, address
    ./applab.sh pods              # the pods, and why one is not ready
    ./applab.sh config            # env vars, and the names of the secrets
    ./applab.sh replicas 3        # how many copies to run (no argument: print it)
    ./applab.sh env set K=V ...   # set environment variables
    ./applab.sh env unset K ...   # remove them
    ./applab.sh secret set K=V ...  # set secret values
    ./applab.sh secret unset K ...  # remove them
    ./applab.sh restart           # roll the pods, keeping the image
    ./applab.sh stop              # remove the running app, keeping the source
    ./applab.sh key               # this app's key

Two of these differ from what you might expect, and both are the console's
behaviour too:

- **`replicas` takes effect on the next deploy**, not immediately. It changes
  the app's setting, and the deployer is what applies it — the same way the
  app's port and image work. Set it, then `./applab.sh deploy`.
- **`deploy` builds if it has to.** A commit with an image reuses it; one that
  was never built is built first. That is so deploying a commit you just pushed
  is one command rather than two.

`stop` keeps the source and history, so a deploy brings the app back. `delete`
does not, which is why it takes the id as an argument and makes you type it.

**Keep it current.** Both files are written into this repository every time
source is uploaded, so a checkout that has not been pushed in a while carries a
copy from whenever it last was — and AppLab's API changes between releases. If a
command here fails in a way that suggests the deployment has moved on, refresh
them from the running server:

    ./applab.sh self-update

That fetches the current `applab.sh` and `AGENT.md`, which is exactly what the
next upload would write — including the two defaults above, so a working
checkout keeps working.

## The API, if you would rather not use the script

Every call takes `Authorization: Bearer $APPLAB_KEY`. Every response is
`{"data": ...}` on success and `{"error": "..."}` with a non-2xx status on
failure; a 2xx means it worked. `GET $APPLAB_URL/api/v1/describe` lists them all
with the credential each one needs.

**Upload source** — a tar or tar.gz of the project, as the raw request body.
This is the one call that puts code in.

    tar czf - --exclude=./.git . \
      | curl -sS -X POST "$APPLAB_URL/api/v1/apps/{{APP}}/source?message=<note>" \
          -H "Authorization: Bearer $APPLAB_KEY" \
          -H "Content-Type: application/gzip" \
          --data-binary @-

A single wrapping directory is stripped, so `tar czf - myproject` lands with the
project's contents at the root. A source tree identical to the current tip is a
no-op — it returns the existing commit rather than adding an empty one.

**Build it** — from a commit, or from the current tip if `commit_sha` is omitted.

    curl -sS -X POST "$APPLAB_URL/api/v1/apps/{{APP}}/builds" \
      -H "Authorization: Bearer $APPLAB_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
      -d '{"commit_sha":"<sha>"}'

A build runs as a Job in the cluster and returns immediately. The image is built
from the `Dockerfile` in the source — the one requirement AppLab puts on a
project.

**Deploy it** — names a commit and returns the address it is served at.

    curl -sS -X POST "$APPLAB_URL/api/v1/apps/{{APP}}/deploy" \
      -H "Authorization: Bearer $APPLAB_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
      -d '{"commit_sha":"<sha>"}'

A deploy of a commit that was never built fails with 409 and names the fix rather
than quietly building first — pass `{"build":true}` to have it build and deploy
in one call. A commit that *was* built has its image reused, so deploying an
earlier commit is a rollback.

**Watch it** — the two calls worth knowing:

    curl -sS "$APPLAB_URL/api/v1/apps/{{APP}}/status"  -H "Authorization: Bearer $APPLAB_KEY"
    curl -sS "$APPLAB_URL/api/v1/apps/{{APP}}/logs?follow=false&tail=500" -H "Authorization: Bearer $APPLAB_KEY"

`status` reports what the cluster has: whether a Deployment exists, how many
replicas are ready, the image it runs and the commit that image was built from.
Nothing is recorded on AppLab's side, so there is no second answer to reconcile —
the cluster is the only one.

`GET /api/v1/apps/{{APP}}/diagnose` answers "why is it not working" in one call —
pods, events and the relevant log, most likely cause first. Reach for it before
reading the individual endpoints.

## Configuration travels with the code

Environment variables and secrets are set through the API and take effect on the
next deploy, not immediately — configuration follows the same path as source.

    curl -sS -X PUT "$APPLAB_URL/api/v1/apps/{{APP}}/env" \
      -H "Authorization: Bearer $APPLAB_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
      -d '{"env":{"LOG_LEVEL":"debug"}}'

Secrets are set the same way at `.../secrets`. A secret's value can never be read
back — not through this API, not from the console, not from the CLI. Setting one
again replaces it, and reading the app's configuration returns the *names* of its
secrets and nothing more.

"The API will not return it" is not the same as "it is hidden". Both kinds of value
are written into the Deployment's `env` in the clear, so anyone who can read a
Deployment in this namespace can read every secret in it. The distinction is worth
keeping straight for the person who has to decide what to put in one.

`PORT` is refused as an environment variable: it is derived from the app's
configured port.

## Three things that are easy to get wrong

**A deploy is not a build.** They are separate calls on purpose, so a build
failure is never reported as a deploy failure, and so a rollback cannot fail for
a reason the original build did not.

**The seeded files are AppLab's, not yours.** `applab.sh` and `AGENT.md` are
written on every upload, so an edit to either is gone with the next push. The
current versions are servable too — `GET $APPLAB_URL/api/v1/apps/{{APP}}/agent/files`
lists them and each has its own URL — which is what `./applab.sh self-update`
uses.

**The source is a real git repository.** It can be cloned with any git client —
the key goes in as the password, any username works:

    git clone "https://x:$APPLAB_KEY@<host>/git/{{APP}}.git"

That address is on the app's page in the console. Everything in the history is
deployable, which is what makes a rollback possible.

**There can be more than one branch, and one of them runs.** The URL above is the
active branch, and another branch is the same URL with the branch after an `@`:

    git push  "https://x:$APPLAB_KEY@<host>/git/{{APP}}@dev.git" HEAD:dev
    git clone "https://x:$APPLAB_KEY@<host>/git/{{APP}}@dev.git"

Pushing is what creates a branch — git's own rule — and cloning one that does not
exist fails as "repository not found". Switching which branch runs is
`./applab.sh branch use <name>`, and it **replaces what is serving**: one app runs
one branch at a time, so this deploys the branch it switches to rather than
adding a second thing.

Each branch is stored as a repository of its own, so an app with several branches
uses roughly that many times the storage. That is a platform-level cost, not
something to work around from in here — just worth knowing before pushing a dozen
branches of a large project.
